<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>VibeSDR Buddy</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<!-- ★★★ FROM THE PROJECT SETTING, exactly as the phone's plist is — and for the reason written
	     there. This was a LITERAL, and it had not moved since build 170: the phone went to 209
	     today and the watch app claimed 170 every single time. Two sources of truth for one
	     number, and the one that mattered was the one nobody edited.
	     ★★★ THE COST IS WORSE HERE THAN ON THE PHONE. watchOS decides whether to replace an
	     installed app by its build number, so an unchanged one means the install has nothing to
	     do — sometimes it lands anyway, sometimes it sits and spins (Stuart, 2026-08-28: "buddy not
	     installing to the watch it is just spinning ... every other attempt buddy install worked
	     straight away"). Flaky in exactly the way a no-op dressed as an action is flaky.
	     ★★ AND WHEN IT "WORKED", IT MAY HAVE INSTALLED THE SAME BINARY AGAIN — which is the far
	     more expensive half: a whole evening of Buddy fixes tested against a watch app that had no
	     reason to change. The phone's own note says it: "you test yesterday's code believing it is
	     today's."
	     ★ CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION is set on BOTH targets (four build configurations), so the watch
	     now moves with the phone and Xcode Cloud's increment reaches it. -->
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<!-- The FM-DX (servers.fmdx.org) and KiwiSDR (rx.linkfanel.net) directory APIs are http-only,
		     which watchOS ATS blocks by default — so those directories wouldn't load. The phone app
		     already allows this; the watch needs the same. -->
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
	<key>WKApplication</key>
	<true/>
	<key>WKCompanionAppBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>com.vibesdr.app</string>
	<key>WKRunsIndependentlyOfCompanionApp</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
